WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.780
Too much lead or too little makes every dive harder.

2
00:00:04.587 --> 00:00:06.617
The answer is a balanced rig.

3
00:00:07.717 --> 00:00:10.697
A rig is balanced when it passes two checks.

4
00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:14.498
At the end, you stay at the last stop without effort.

5
00:00:14.498 --> 00:00:17.768
At the start, you can ascend by swimming if the wing fails.

6
00:00:20.068 --> 00:00:28.098
For example, our rig has a five millimetre wetsuit, an eleven point one litre aluminium tank and a thirty pound wing.

7
00:00:29.848 --> 00:00:32.648
Step one: let's find the lead you need.

8
00:00:33.535 --> 00:00:39.235
At three metres, with fifty bar and an empty wing, change the lead until you stay without effort.

9
00:00:39.235 --> 00:00:41.205
Our rig needs four kilograms.

10
00:00:43.505 --> 00:00:47.705
Step two: let's find how heavy the rig is at the start.

11
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:57.290
At thirty metres, the full tank has about two kilograms more air and the wetsuit is compressed, so our rig is about four point six kilograms heavy.

12
00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:04.280
Most divers can ascend by swimming with a rig about five kilograms heavy, but test your own limit.

13
00:01:05.055 --> 00:01:10.065
The rule: if you cannot, change the gear or carry part of the lead where you can drop it.

14
00:01:12.365 --> 00:01:19.205
Step three: let's find where the lead goes, because where it sits changes your trim, the angle of your body.

15
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.890
Move the lead and the heavy items until you stay horizontal, with your hands and fins still.

16
00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.790
Weights Check records each check, for each rig and water.

17
00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.370
Its balance shows the values for both checks on one screen.

18
00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:36.850
Free on the App Store.

19
00:01:37.653 --> 00:01:39.063
diver dot today.
